  "narration": {
    "transcript": "The ultimate vocal warm-up. One idea ties it together: balance, not force. Narrow the passage - hum, buzz, or sing through a straw - and a cushion of air holds your folds in the ideal posture. Stage one: body and breath; loosen the frame, stand tall, breathe low, hiss six to eight seconds. Stage two: wake the voice gently with a soft hum. Stage three, the core: straw slides, bubbles, lip trills and ng sirens for five to seven minutes - never sing to the bottom of your breath. Stage four, clean closure: vocal fry blooming into tone, bright nay arpeggios, and messa di voce - soft to loud to soft on one note. Stage five: open the range with long sirens, gliding lightly across the bridge. Stage six: resonance - mah may mee moh moo, ringing in the mask. Stage seven: wake the words. Stage eight: bridge into your song, easy verse first, hardest phrase last. Short on time? Straw, trills, fry, nay, one siren, one phrase. All night? Finish warming fifteen to thirty minutes before you sing, speak light, sip room-temperature water, then cool down with soft descending sirens. Balance, not force. Now go and sing beautifully."
  },
